@startuml bootwire_write_sequence
!include ../_style.iuml
title <size:21><color:#1B3A5F><b>WRITE</b></color></size>\n<size:13><color:#5B6B7C>one chunk per command; the target programs it, then reads it back</color></size>
!include _participants.iuml

autonumber "<b>00"
APP -> HOST : write_image(image, 0x08008000)
HOST -> HOST : pad with 0xFF to the write alignment\nchunk = (max_payload − 4) rounded down\nto the alignment = 256 bytes
loop for each 256-byte chunk
  HOST -> TP : WRITE [SEQ, address, 256 data bytes]
  activate TP
  TP -> TP : address and length aligned?\ninside the application region?
  alt a check fails
    TP --> HOST : [SEQ, 0x91, <color:#C0392B><b>BAD_PARAM</b></color>]
    HOST --> APP : BootWireStatusError (nothing written)
  else checks pass
    TP -> BL : program the chunk
    BL -> BL : read back and compare with the payload
    alt program error
      TP --> HOST : [SEQ, 0x91, <color:#C0392B><b>FLASH</b></color>]
      HOST --> APP : BootWireStatusError
    else read-back differs (flash was not erased)
      TP --> HOST : [SEQ, 0x91, <color:#C0392B><b>VERIFY</b></color>]
      HOST --> APP : BootWireStatusError
    else written correctly
      TP --> HOST : [SEQ, 0x91, <color:#1E8449><b>OK</b></color>]
      deactivate TP
      HOST -> APP : progress("write", done, total)
    end
  end
end
note over HOST, BL
  WRITE carries its own address, so the target keeps no transfer state.
  A lost or repeated chunk can never land at the wrong place.
end note
@enduml
